# What the image build is allowed to see, which is not the media library.
#
# There was no .dockerignore, so `COPY . .` sent the whole working directory -
# and `library/` is a bind mount whose entire purpose is to hold tens of
# gigabytes. After a drop of 94 audiobooks a build reported
# "transferring context: 8.87GB" and then died with an RPC EOF, having written
# all of it into BuildKit's cache on the same drive the library lives on. The
# drive hit 0 bytes free, and uploads then failed with EIO, which is what Docker
# Desktop reports for a full bind mount. One missing file, two symptoms that look
# unrelated.
#
# An allow-list rather than a deny-list, deliberately. A deny-list has to name
# every directory that might one day be enormous, and the one that already was
# had been sitting there unlisted since the first commit. This way a new folder
# of media cannot creep back in, and the failure mode if the build ever needs
# something new is "file not found" at build time - loud, immediate, and nothing
# like a full disk a week later.
*

# The Go module, which has no dependencies and therefore no go.sum.
!go.mod

# The binary, and everything embedded into it: the web UI under
# internal/webui/assets and the starter library under internal/starter/media.
!cmd
!internal

# Named explicitly. BuildKit handles the Dockerfile itself, but being excluded
# by a wildcard is not a thing to leave to the builder's good manners.
!Dockerfile
